<section class="faq-section" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
  <hr class="seo-divider" style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
  <h2 class="faq-section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <p class="faq-section-sub">Everything students ask about CGPA, percentage, GPA conversion and grading.</p>

  <div class="faq-group-label">How to Convert</div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">How to convert CGPA to percentage?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Multiply your CGPA by 9.5 using the standard CBSE formula: <strong>Percentage = CGPA × 9.5</strong>. For example, 8.0 CGPA × 9.5 = 76%. If your university uses a different formula (SPPU uses piecewise grade-band conversion per Circular 332/2020, VTU uses CGPA × 10), apply the institution-specific rule instead.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">How to calculate percentage from CGPA?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Step 1: Find your CGPA on your grade card. Step 2: Identify your university's formula (CBSE: ×9.5 | SPPU: piecewise by grade band | VTU: ×10). Step 3: Apply the formula. Step 4: The result is your approximate equivalent percentage. For formal submissions, always collect a percentage equivalence certificate from your university registrar.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">How to calculate CGPA from percentage?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Reverse the standard formula: <strong>CGPA = Percentage ÷ 9.5</strong>. So 76% ÷ 9.5 = 8.0 CGPA. For SPPU: use the piecewise inverse based on grade band. For VTU: CGPA = Percentage ÷ 10. Use the "% → CGPA" tab in the calculator above and select your university.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">How to convert percentage to CGPA?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Divide your percentage by 9.5 to get your CGPA on a 10-point scale (CBSE standard). Example: 85% ÷ 9.5 = 8.95 CGPA. For a 4-point GPA scale, divide your percentage by 25: 85% ÷ 25 = 3.4 GPA. The calculator on this page handles all conversions automatically.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">How to convert CGPA to percentage out of 4?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">If your CGPA is on a 4.0 scale (US system), convert to percentage using: <strong>Percentage = (CGPA ÷ 4) × 100</strong>. So 3.5 out of 4.0 = 87.5%. To go the other direction (percentage → GPA out of 4), use: GPA = (Percentage ÷ 100) × 4.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group-label">Specific CGPA Values</div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">What is 7.6 CGPA in percentage?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">7.6 CGPA equals <strong>72.2%</strong> using the CBSE formula (×9.5), <strong>68.5%</strong> under SPPU (Grade A band: 10×7.6−7.5), and <strong>76%</strong> under VTU (×10). The result varies by university, so always apply your institution's prescribed formula.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">What is 60% in CGPA?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">60% corresponds to <strong>6.32 CGPA</strong> using CBSE (60÷9.5), <strong>6.75 CGPA</strong> under SPPU (Grade A band inverse: (60+7.5)÷10), and <strong>6.0 CGPA</strong> under VTU (60÷10). At most Indian universities, 60% or 6.0 CGPA marks the First Class threshold.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Does 8 CGPA mean 80 percentage?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Not always. Under VTU and KTU (which use CGPA × 10), 8 CGPA does equal exactly 80%. However, under the standard CBSE formula it equals <strong>76%</strong> (8 × 9.5), and under SPPU (Grade A band) it equals <strong>72.5%</strong> (10 × 8 − 7.5). The "8 CGPA = 80%" assumption only holds for universities using a direct ×10 multiplier.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is 95% equal to 10 CGPA?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Under the CBSE formula, 10 CGPA × 9.5 = <strong>95%</strong>, so yes — 95% is the equivalent of a perfect 10.0 CGPA on the CBSE scale. However under VTU, 10 CGPA = 100%, and under SPPU (Grade O band), 10 CGPA = 20×10−100 = 100%. The 95% figure is specific to the CBSE/generic system.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">What does an 8.2 CGPA mean?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">8.2 CGPA is a strong academic performance, equivalent to <strong>77.9%</strong> (CBSE), <strong>74.5%</strong> (SPPU Grade A band), or <strong>82%</strong> (VTU). It falls in the Excellent range, typically qualifying as First Class with Distinction at most universities. For placement purposes, 8.2 CGPA comfortably clears the cut-offs of most top IT and engineering companies.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group-label">Is My CGPA Good?</div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is a 7.2 CGPA good?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Yes, 7.2 CGPA is generally considered good. It translates to about 68.4% (CBSE) or 64.5% (SPPU Grade A band) — solidly in First Class territory. Most IT companies (TCS, Infosys, Wipro) require a minimum of 6.0–6.5 CGPA, so 7.2 clears those bars comfortably. For product-based or core engineering roles, a 7.5+ is often preferred.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is 8 CGPA good or bad?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">8.0 CGPA is very good. It corresponds to 76% (CBSE) or 72.5% (SPPU Grade A band), placing you in the First Class with Distinction bracket at most universities. It is above the cut-off for the vast majority of campus placements in India and is a competitive score for graduate admissions abroad (roughly 3.2 on a 4.0 GPA scale).</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is 7.5 CGPA low?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">No, 7.5 CGPA is not low — it is above average and clears the eligibility bar for most campus placements. It equals about 71.25% (CBSE) or 67.5% (SPPU Grade A band). Many students consider 7.5 the "safe" minimum for placement eligibility at reputed companies. It only appears low when competing for highly selective roles that prefer 8.0+ CGPA.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is 6.5 a bad CGPA?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">6.5 CGPA is not bad — it equals roughly 61.75% (CBSE) or 57.5% (SPPU Grade A band), which qualifies as First Class at most universities. It clears the 60% threshold for many government jobs and basic IT company requirements. However, for premium placements or postgraduate admissions with scholarships, a higher CGPA (7.5+) is typically expected.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is 8.7 CGPA good in engineering?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Yes, 8.7 CGPA is excellent in engineering. It equals roughly 82.65% (CBSE) or 79.5% (SPPU Grade A band), placing you well above the First Class with Distinction threshold. It is competitive for GATE, top PSU placements, and international MS admissions. In campus placement terms, 8.7 CGPA makes you eligible for virtually every company including product-based firms with high cut-offs.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group-label">Class, Grades &amp; Pass/Fail</div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is a 6.0 CGPA first class?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Yes, at most Indian universities a 6.0 CGPA qualifies as First Class. Under VTU (×10), 6.0 CGPA = 60%, which is the typical First Class boundary. Under SPPU (Grade A band: 10×6.0−7.5 = 52.5%), which falls in the Second Class range — so it depends on your university. Always verify the class definitions in your university's ordinance.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Is 4 CGPA pass or fail?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">On a 10-point Indian scale, 4.0 CGPA equals about 38% (CBSE) or 32.5% (SPPU Grade D/P band) — which is typically a <strong>fail</strong>, as most universities require a minimum of 40–45% to pass. On a 4.0 GPA scale however, 4.0 is a perfect score. Context — which scale your 4 is on — is critical here.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group-label">Jobs &amp; Career</div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">Can I get a job with 7 CGPA?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">Yes, absolutely. A 7.0 CGPA clears the eligibility bar for most major IT companies including TCS, Infosys, Wipro, Cognizant, and Capgemini. Many service-based and mid-tier product companies set their cut-off at 6.0–6.5 CGPA. For top-tier product companies (Google, Microsoft, Amazon), CGPA is less critical than problem-solving skills, but a 7.0 CGPA will not disqualify you from applying.</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group-label">GPA on 4.0 Scale</div>

  <div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <button class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)" itemprop="name">What is a 7.2 GPA on a 4.0 scale?<span class="faq-icon">+</span></button>
    <div class="faq-a" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><div itemprop="text">A 7.2 on a 4.0 scale is not possible — you likely mean 7.2 CGPA on a 10-point Indian scale. Converted to a 4.0 GPA: (7.2 ÷ 10) × 4 = <strong>2.88 GPA</strong>. This is slightly below the 3.0 benchmark preferred by US graduate programmes, though many universities still consider applications from students with 2.7–3.0 GPA if other credentials are strong.</div></div>
  </div>

</section>

